# Metal/covalent organic frameworks for aqueous rechargeable <sc>zinc-ion</sc> batteries

## Metal/covalent organic frameworks for aqueous rechargeable zinc-ion batteries
Hu Hong, Xun Guo, Jiaxiong Zhu, Zhuoxi Wu, Qing Li & Chunyi Zhi
Department of Materials Science and Engineering, City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong 999077, China
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have become one of the most successful energy storage systems due to their high operating voltage, high energy density, and long cycle life. However, with the widespread use of LIBs in recent decades, lithium resources are at risk of being exhausted. Therefore, it is necessary to find a substitute for LIBs to meet the needs of future large-scale energy storage systems. Because of their competitiveness, low cost, and high safety, aqueous rechargeable zinc-ion batteries (ARZIBs) are regarded as promising components in the post-lithium-ion-battery era. Given the tunable composition, ordered porous channels, and controllable structure of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs), these frameworks are viewed as potential materials for developing high-performance ARZIBs. In this review, we focus on the recent developments in the applications of MOF-/COF-based materials in ARZIBs, including in electrode materials, anode modifications, separators, and solid electrolytes. We then focus on the critical factors and optimization techniques of MOF-/COFbased materials that affect the performance of ARZIBs. Finally, we conclude with some projections for the expansion of ARZIBs containing MOF-/COF-based materials.
metal/covalent organic frameworks, aqueous zinc-ion batteries, electrode materials, anode modifications, separators, electrolytes
Citation: Hong H, Guo X, Zhu J, Wu Z, Li Q, Zhi C. Metal/covalent organic frameworks for aqueous rechargeable zinc-ion batteries. SciChinaChem, 2024, 67: 247–259, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-023-1558-2
## 1 Introduction
Regarded as the most widely studied electrochemical energy conversion systems, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been used in a variety of energy storage devices [1]. However, their development for large-scale energy storage applications has been largely hindered by their inherent shortcomings, such as their high cost, toxic electrolytes, and safety concerns, as well as the scarcity of lithium resources [2]. Given the low cost, high safety, easy operation, and environmental friendliness of aqueous rechargeable batteries, these batteries have been regarded as viable options for large-scale electrochemical energy storage [3–6]. In general, the ionic conductivity of aqueous electrolytes is typically 100 times greater than that of nonaqueous electrolytes, thereby presenting an opportunity for the development of energy storage devices with superior rate capability [7]. Several aqueous batteries based on monovalent alkaline metal cations (e.g., ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+},{\mathrm{Na}}^{+}$ , and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ ) and multivalent charge carriers (e.g., $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) have been reported [8–11]. Among these batteries, aqueous rechargeable zinc-ion batteries (ARZIBs) with relatively neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ electrolytes, in which Zn metal is used as the anode because of its relatively low redox potential $(-0.76\mathrm{~V~}\nu s\$ . standard hydrogen electrode), high theoretical capacity (approximately $5,855\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ ), and low cost, are particularly promising [3,12–14]. Following the use of metallic $Z\mathrm{n}$ for the first time in a battery in 1799 by Alessandro Volta and his colleagues, considerable research has been conducted on ARZIBs, including on Zn anode modifications, electrolyte design, and cathode material exploration (e.g., vanadium-based oxides, manganese-based oxides, Prussian blue analogues, and organic quinone analogues) [15–18]. Despite the progress, the practical applications of ARZIBs have severely hindered by numerous difficult problems.
As essential components of ARZIBs, electrode materials play a critical role in enhancing energy density [5,19,20]. Although the cathode materials that are commonly used in ARZIBs, such as manganese- and vanadium-based oxides, have a high capacity $(>300\mathrm{\mAh\g}^{-1},$ , they exhibit an unsatisfactory voltage plateau [21,22]. In addition, although Prussian blue analogues have a high discharge platform $(>1.5\mathrm{V})$ , their specific capacity does not exceed $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , which is inconvenient in practical applications [17]. By contrast, Zn metal anodes have a high energy density, which is essential for achieving high battery performance and long battery lifetime [23]. Zn dendrite growth and parasitic reactions (e.g., hydrogen evolution and Zn anode corrosion) drastically reduce the lifetime and Coulombic efficiency (CE) of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes, thereby posing a serious threat to the operation stability of batteries [24]. Therefore, modern research has focused on the development of advanced materials that are capable of improving the electrochemical performance of ARZIBs.
Because of the tunable composition, diverse functional groups, highly ordered porous channels, easily controllable structure, and high surface area of metal-organic framework (MOF) and covalent organic framework (COF) materials, these materials have attracted considerable research attention [25–27]. In MOF-/COF-based materials, both metal nodes and organic ligands could function as redox centers; thus, these materials have exhibited the satisfactory performance in LIBs [28]. In contrast to conventional inorganic materials, MOFs and COFs have a diversified and controllable structure that offers high potential for the development of highperformance electrode materials [29]. In addition, because of the well-aligned pores of MOFs/COFs, ions can be rapidly transported in them, which lays the foundation for MOF-/ COF-based solid electrolytes and separators [30]. Because of the limited pore size of MOFs/COFs, they confine and sieve ions and molecules to tune the electrolyte solvation structure and stabilize the deposition of Zn ions, which allows the problems of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes such as hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and corrosion to be solved [31]. Recently, many studies have reported the use of MOF-/COF-based materials in ARZIBs (Figure 1). In this review, we summarize the applications of MOFs and COFs in ARZIBs, including in cathodes, anodes, electrolytes, and separators. We then describe the difficulties associated with the use of MOF-/COFbased materials in ARZIBs and the potential future applications of these materials in ARZIBs. This review provides researchers with useful information for the development of high-performance ARZIBs.
## 2 Applications of MOFs/COFs in aqueous ARZIBs
## 2.1 MOF-/COF-based electrode materials
When used as electrode materials in ARZIBs, MOFs typically have two types of active redox sites: metal nodes or clusters and organic ligands [28,32,33]. During the cycling process, the metal centers of MOFs serve as host sites to accommodate the insertion of carrier ions through reversible valence changes [34]. Moreover, the open channels and pores improve penetration and accelerate the ion transport process, which allows MOFs to provide more active sites than those provided by traditional metal oxides [35,36]. The electrode performance can be adjusted by selecting different metal sites or clusters and controlling their coordination environment. By contrast, organic ligands in MOFs serve as redox-active sites [37–39]. Similar to organic electrode materials, the redox activity of ligands depends on the redox capacity of the functional groups (e.g., carbonyl, nitro, and nitrogen groups) [39–42]. Compared with small-molecule organic electrode materials, the organic ligands in MOFs are bridged with metal sites, thereby forming a three-dimensional macromolecular structure, which not only inhibits the dissolution of the intermediate states of discharge but also alleviates the volume expansion of ion intercalation and deintercalation during the charging or discharging process [43,44]. However, during the electrochemical process, the electrochemical environment of the coordination bonds generated between the metal sites and organic ligands in MOFs typically changes, thereby causing the metal ions to dissolve and the structure to collapse [33,45].
In contrast to MOFs, COF materials are spliced by forming strong covalent bonds between organic building units [46– 48]. Covalent bonds are typically characterized by high chemical and electrochemical stability, especially in aqueous environments, which results in increased cycling stability in COF materials [48–50]. MOF and COF materials provide a variety of options for the precise molecular manipulation of electrochemically active components in energy storage applications. Although these materials exhibit high potential as electrode materials for ARZIBs, they are still in their infancy.
In 2019, Nam et al. [51] reported a two-dimensional conductive MOF called $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ , which can be used as a zinc-ion battery cathode (Figure 2a). Hydrated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions can be inserted into the host MOF structure through a unique one-dimensional channel topology to enhance the diffusion of ions and achieve a low interfacial resistance. They reported that the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ cathode had an unprecedented reversible capacity of $288\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $50\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (Figure 2b). They also reported that the pseudocapacitance mechanism was responsible for the excellent rate performance of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ cathode. And they offered novel insights into the fabrication of high-performance MOF electrodes. Although they achieved a high capacity, the cycling stability remained suboptimal (below $75\%$ of the initial capacity after 100 cycles, Figure 2c), possibly because of the structural dissociation caused by the involvement of the metal center in the redox reaction. In 2019, He et al. [52] constructed a three-dimensional conductive vanadium-based MOF (VMOF) array on carbon nanotube fibers through a timedependent solvothermal reaction (Figure 2d). They reported that their V-MOF had an excellent rate capability because of its hierarchical porous structure and high conductivity, thereby achieving a specific capacity of $66\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $5,000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (Figure 2e). They also discovered that the valence state of the central vanadium metal element did not change during the intercalation and deintercalation of the zinc ions. In conclusion, the aforementioned findings confirm that maintaining the stability of the central metal during electrochemical processes is useful for achieving long-term cycling stability [53]. Furthermore, the combination of
MOFs with conductive substrates is an effective strategy to enhance the overall conductivity and thus enhance the rate capability.
In 2020, Xu et al. [54] investigated the electrochemical Zn storage mechanism of four MOFs: Mn(BTC), Fe(BDC), $\mathrm{Co(BDC)}$ , and V(BDC) (1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid denoted as BTC and 1,4-dicarboxybenzene denoted as BDC, respectively). They reported that among these MOFs, $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{BTC})$ had the highest $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ storage performance, with a specific capacity of $112\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Further investigations revealed that $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{BTC})$ was converted into $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(B T C)}}$ after cycling. In addition, the cyclic voltammetry curve of $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{BTC})$ had a shape similar to that of $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ , which confirmed that energy storage was dominated by $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ (Figure 2f). However, because of the low electrical conductivity of the $Z_{\mathrm{{n(BTC)}}}$ and $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ mixture and the negligible capacity of $Z_{\mathrm{{n(BTC)}}}$ , the $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{BTC})$ cathode exhibited inferior rate performance to that of pristine $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ (Figure 2g). In addition, the cycling stability of the Mn(BTC) cathode deteriorated with each cycle, as shown in Figure 2h. Therefore, they concluded that the aforementioned conversion-type battery mechanism is typically associated with low battery performance because MOFs readily decompose during redox reactions. They also noted that although the aforementioned by-products exhibited certain electrochemical performance, the widely distributed inactive components reduced the overall performance of the electrode.
Because of the abundant redox-active centers, weak solubility, and high electrochemical stability of COFs, these frameworks have been widely examined as electrode materials for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ storage [55,56]. Khayum et al. [57] synthesized a two-dimensional COF by linking 2,5-diaminohydroquinone dihydrochloride and 1,3,5-triformylpholoroglucinol (HqTp-COF) as a cathode material for ARZIBs (Figure 3a). By using ex-situ X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, ex-situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, the aforementioned authors reported that the functionalities of $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ in polymers worked in tandem with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to cause a high capacity of $276\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to be achieved at a current density of $125\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ (Figure 3b). They also reported that the zinc ions reversibly interacted with the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bonds during the electrochemical redox reaction, which provided HqTp-COF with excellent cycling stability (Figure 3c). Wang et al. [49] synthesized a novel phenanthroline COF (PA-COF) as a cathode material for ARZIBs (Figure
3d). They reported that phenanthroline allowed PA-COF to achieve $Z n^{2+}~(60\%)$ and $\mathrm{~H~}^{+}~(40\%)$ co-intercalation, which resulted in a high capacity of $247\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . The aforementioned authors also revealed that the electron-rich backbone of PA-COF with heteroatoms and nitrogen-rich active sites not only guaranteed a high specific capacity but also helped achieve an excellent long-cycle capability (capacity decay of only $0.38\%$ per cycle over a total of 10,000 cycles, Figure 3e). They subsequently discovered that the introduction of a quinone group into 1,4,5,8,9,12-hexaazatriphenylene-based COFs considerably improved their performance [58]. These findings confirmed that increasing the redox-active components in COFs effectively increases the electrochemical performance of COF-based electrodes. In a similar study, Liu et al. [45] synthesized a dual-active-site, two-dimensional COF (Tp-PTO-COF) with adjacent carbonyl groups and $\beta$ -keto carbonyl groups to achieve reversible $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion storage. They reported that, because of the abundance of nucleophilic carbonyl active sites, Tp-PTO-COF had a high specific capacity of $301.4\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $200\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$
In addition to the aforementioned COF-based cathode, Yu et al. [59] designed a two-dimensional polyarylimide (PI)
COF as an anode material for ARZIBs (Figure 3f). They reported that the well-aligned COF pores of PI were responsible for the high accessibility of the redox-active carbonyls and efficient ion diffusion with minimal energy barriers, which resulted in a capacity of $332\mathrm{C}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $700\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ ) and a long cycle life (retention of $85\%$ over 4,000 cycles, Figure 3h). They also reported that the aforementioned COF-based anode $(-0.4\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. standard hydrogen electrode) can be used as an alternative to zinc metal anodes. Notably, compared with MOFs, COFs generally exhibit better cycling stability because the covalent bonds between their two linking units do not undergo redox reactions during the electrochemical processes. However, in MOFs, when the metal center participates in the redox process, its coordination environment changes accordingly, leading to the dissolution of metal ions and further capacity fading. Therefore, in order to improve the cycling stability, it is recommended to select metal nodes that do not participate in the redox to construct MOFs.
## 2.2 Anode modification
Zinc metal anodes have attracted considerable attention as an essential component of ARZIBs [3,60]. However, these anodes are still associated with numerous problems, mainly (1) the zinc dendrites and dead zinc caused by uncontrolled zinc deposition and (2) the irreversible side reactions between the zinc anode and the electrolyte, such as the HER and corrosion that occur during Zn plating or stripping [23,24,61]. These phenomena might result in a low CE, low cycling stability, and even short-circuiting in ARZIBs [62]. As crystalline materials with highly tunable organic components, pore sizes, and ordered channels, MOFs and COFs have been reported to modulate zinc deposition and promote zinc anode protection [36,43,63].
Zinc anodes have been commonly coated with a MOF/ COF layer. For example, Fan et al. [64] coated a Zn anode with a negatively charged protection layer containing an MOF $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{)}$ and a flexible sulfonated poly(ether ether ketone) binder (USL) (Figure 4a and 4b). They reported that this MOF-based protective layer not only suppressed dendrite formation and side reactions but also helped adjust the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ flux and achieve uniform Zn deposition. They also reported that the zincophilic $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ functional groups on the MOFs helped modify the local electrolyte structure on the anode side, thereby preventing HER and passivation (Figure 4c). Consequently, at a current density of $5.0\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a high $Z\mathrm{n}$ plating/stripping efficiency of $99.34\%$ was attained at 600 cycles, and dendrite-free cycles were obtained for more than $700\mathrm{{h}}$ . The rate performance and long-term cycling lifespan of $Z\mathrm{n}||\mathrm{VO}_{2}$ complete cells were also improved.
The surface of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes contains highly active water molecules, which are the primary cause for HER and subsequent corrosion and passivation. Zhou et al. [65] proposed an MOF-based coating technique to tune solvated $\left[Z\mathrm{n(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\right]^{2+}$ species and address these problems. They reported that the concentration of free water molecules considerably decreased in high-concentration electrolytes, which in turn increased the stability of the metal electrode. As shown in
Figure 4d, the aforementioned authors detected two ions with distinct solvation structures, namely $[\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}]$ and $[{Z}{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{O}})_{5}{\mathrm{OSO}}_{3}^{2-}]$ , in a saturated zinc sulfate solution (approximately $3.3~\mathrm{mol~L}^{-1}$ ). By using Raman spectroscopy, they reported that the MOFs allowed for the entry of $[\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5}\mathrm{OSO}_{3}^{2-}]$ while restricted the entry of $[Z{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}]$ because of its limited pore size (approximately $2.94\mathrm{\AA}$ , Figure 4e). Consequently, they observed a supersaturated front surface electrolyte layer on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode, which allowed the symmetric $Z\mathrm{n}$ cell to operate for more than $^{3,000\mathrm{h}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ without forming dendrites (Figure 4f). Overall, they provided insights into the development of MOF-based coating layers for the protection of Zn anodes. Wang et al. [66] used a $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ MOF coating layer containing a filled hydrophobic organic electrolyte $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ -tris(2,2,2-trifluoroethyl)-phosphate) to separate aqueous electrolytes from organic electrolytes (Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ and 4h). They reported that organophilic MOFs strongly confined the organic electrolyte to prevent the penetration of water into the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface. Even when a small number of water molecules entered the organic phase, they were blocked by the solid electrolyte interface (SEI) layer that originated from the reduction of the organic electrolyte (Figure 4i and 4j). Therefore, the aforementioned technique not only completely eliminated the water-induced HER, corrosion, and passivation of the Zn anode but also aided in the formation of a strong SEI layer to prevent the growth of Zn dendrites. With these advantages, the organic electrolyte@MOF-protected Zn anode achieved a high CE of
$99.1\%$ over 350 cycles. In addition, the assembled $Z\mathrm{n}\|\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ full cells exhibited a capacity of $141\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 600 cycles and a retention of $97.2\%$ .
In addition to MOFs, COFs have often been used to modify zinc anodes because of their straightforward preparation process, structural tunability, high porosity, and high chemical stability. For instance, Hu et al. [67] synthesized a two-dimensional COF containing rich alkynyl units for the protection of $Z\mathrm{n}$ anodes (Figure 5a). They reported that this COF-based coating layer allowed for the homogeneous distribution of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in ordered channels, thus preventing the surface corrosion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ metal and effectively limiting the growth of $Z\mathrm{n}$ dendrites (Figure 5b). They also reported that compared with a bare Zn anode, the COF-coated $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode exhibited higher stability over $900\mathrm{{h}}$ at a current density of
$3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Zhao et al. [70] synthesized a zoophilic COF film $\mathrm{(TpPa{\mathrm{-}}S O_{3}H@Z n)}$ to stabilize a $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode. They reported that the sulfonic acid groups of the film worked in tandem with the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions to achieve uniform $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ deposition and prevent the formation of dendrites. They also reported that the $\mathrm{TpPa}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ film was able to release a large number of protons $\boldsymbol{(\mathrm{H}^{+})}$ to achieve a dynamic equilibrium with $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ , thereby controlling the desolvation of $\left[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\right]^{2+}$ and preventing adverse side effects. Consequently, $Z\mathrm{n}||Z\mathrm{n}$ symmetrical cells with a COF-protected Zn anode ran stably for 600 cycles and maintained a CE of $99\%$ at a current density of $1.0\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Lan et al. [68] designed a series of zincophilic COFs (Zn-AAn-COF, $Z\mathrm{n}$ -DAAQ-COF, and ZnDAACOF, where AAn stands for 1,5-fiaminoanthraquinone, DAAQ stands for 2,6-diaminoanthraquinone, DAA stands for 2,6-diaminoanthracene) to control $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ flux. On the basis of density functional theory calculations, they reported that the zincophilic COFs (e.g., Zn-AAn-COF) had higher adsorption energy for $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms than that did $Z\mathrm{n}$ foil. The aforementioned authors also reported that the distinct pore shape and electrostatic potential distribution of ZnAAn-COF helped eliminate the solvated water when hydrated zinc ions entered the porous channels of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -AAnCOF (Figure 5c). During the $Z\mathrm{n}$ plating/stripping procedure, they monitored the flux of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ex-situ through gas chromatography mass spectrometry. They reported that a Zn-AAnCOF-based cell produced only 0.002 mmol $\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which was 100 times lower than that produced by bare $Z\mathrm{n}$ (Figure 5d). Therefore, they concluded that the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -AAnCOF-coated $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode achieved high stability over 3,000 cycles at a current density of $20\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in symmetric cells and had improved long-term cycling stability for up to 6,000 cycles at a current density of $2,000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ in the assembled $Z\mathrm{n}\|\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ full cells. They also reported that the COF-based protective layer on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ surface not only inhibited the formation of zinc dendrites and hydrogen evolution but also helped control the deposition of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions. Guo et al. [69] synthesized a two-dimensional porous fluorinated COF (FCOF) film to promote the growth of (002) planes in $Z\mathrm{n}$ crystals during the electrodeposition process (Figure 5e), which was attributable to the numerous electronegative F atoms on the FCOF film exhibiting a strong interaction with the underlying Zn atoms to minimize the surface energy of the Zn (002) planes. They reported that the F-containing nanochannels of the FCOF film facilitated ion transport and prevented electrolyte penetration, thereby increasing corrosion resistance. They also reported that symmetric cells with an $\mathrm{FCOF}@Z\mathrm{n}$ anode demonstrated dendrite-free cycles for over $750\mathrm{h}$ at a current density of $40\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . These Fcontaining COFs lay the foundation for the development of high-areal-capacity full cells under high-Zn-utilization conditions.
All the aforementioned COF-modified layers must be prepared first and then coated on a zinc anode. The COF coating layer prepared by this method lacks strong chemical interaction with the Zn anode and cannot maintain stable interfacial contact during repeated plating/stripping, usually resulting in poor stability. Grzybowski et al. [71] proposed a straightforward and universal dip-coating technique for growing COF layers on Zn foil in-situ. Figure 6a depicts nine COFs made of the same aldehyde precursor, namely 1,3,5- triformylphloroglucinol, and various aromatic amine linkers. According to their results, this simple technique allows for achieving large-scale COF film coating over an area of $30\times12~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figure 6b). In addition, the prepared COF-based coatings enabled effective mass and charge transmission while preventing surface corrosion and large $Z\mathrm{n}$ dendrite formation. Given these advantages, they reported that their batteries exhibited excellent capacity retention for over $420\mathrm{h}$ at a current density of $1.0\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{{cm}}^{-\bar{2}}$ . Overall, the aforementioned straightforward and scalable technique has the potential to advance the commercial application of practical zinc anodes.
## 2.3 Separators and electrolytes
In addition to the anode, electrolytes and separators are essential in modifying the behavior of Zn plating and stripping [72,73]. Because of their ordered porous channels, which allow ion transport, MOFs and COFs have high potential as advanced materials for separators and solid electrolytes [74– 77]. Although numerous efforts have been devoted to the design of advanced MOF-/COF-based separators and solid electrolytes in other secondary battery systems, their application in ARZIBs is still in its infancy. In 2021, Wang et al. [78] reported a Janus separator based on a mixture of a $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conductive MOF and reduced graphene oxide (Figure $\ensuremath{7}\mathrm{a}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{7}\mathrm{c})$ . They reported that this anionic MOF allowed for a uniform $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ flux with well-defined porous tunnels to be achieved and that reduced graphene oxide aided in the transport of electrons during battery operation. Consequently, the resulting $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode achieved stable Zn plating/ stripping performance for over $500\mathrm{{h}}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a high CE of $99.2\%$ . Zhou et al. [79] synthesized an ionic sieve MOF membrane to solve problems related to zinc–iodine $(Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2})$ batteries (Figure 7d). Currently, the main problems with $Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2}$ batteries are the severe side effects on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode and the exceptionally strong barrier of triiodide. The aforementioned authors reported that in addition to functioning as a control channel for the modification of the solvation shell, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC membrane on the Zn surface inhibited the movement of ${\mathrm{I}}_{3}^{-}$ , which considerably reduced the related parasitic side reactions on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode (Figure 7e). Therefore, given the improvements in the iodine cathode and $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode, they concluded that the asassembled $Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2}$ batteries exhibited excellent cycling stability for over 6,000 cycles with a high-capacity retention (approximately $84.6\%$ and a high CE (approximately $99.65\%$ , Figure 7f and $\mathrm{7g}$ ).
Pan et al. [80] synthesized an MOF-based, single-ion $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ solid electrolyte (ZnMOF-808) by using a post-synthetic modified MOF (Figure 8a). They reported that the as-prepared MOF-based solid electrolytes consisted of a fixed anionic porous host and mobile $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions, and the ordered porous channels in the MOF host allowed for rapid ion transport (Figure 8b). According to their results, the activated ${\cal Z}\mathrm{nMOF}{-}808$ exhibited low conductivity (approximately $3{\times}10^{-7}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ only) at room temperature. However, after it absorbed some water molecules, its ionic conductivity increased to $2.1{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and it exhibited an ultralow activation energy of $0.12\mathrm{eV.}$ This finding might be attributable to the shielding effect of water molecules coordinated with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . This ionic transport mechanism is similar to that of aqueous electrolytes. Overall, the aforementioned favorable properties of the MOF-based solid electrolyte allowed $Z\mathrm{n}||\mathrm{V}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}$ full batteries to have excellent performance with a capacity of $125~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ over 250 cycles at a current density of 200 mA g .
Because of their ordered porous structure, diverse functionalities, and structural stability, COFs have been used as solid electrolytes in ARZIBs. For instance, Park et al. [81] synthesized a single-ion conducting COF $(\mathrm{TpPa}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}Z\mathrm{n}_{0.5})$ for ARZIBs (Figure 8c and 8d). They reported that, because of the delocalized sulfonates that were covalently attached to the directional pores, the as-prepared $\mathrm{TpPa}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}Z\mathrm{n}_{0.5}$ exhibited single $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ conduction behavior (ionic conductivity $\c=$ $2.2{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transference numbe ${\scriptstyle:=0.91}$ ) and achieved structural stability through its $\upbeta$ -ketoenamine linkages (Figure 8e). Therefore, given these improvements in ionic conductivity and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transference number, the aforementioned authors reported that their $\mathrm{TpPa}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}$ -based $Z\mathrm{n}||Z\mathrm{n}$ symmetrical cell exhibited excellent cycling stability (over $500\mathrm{{h}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , Figure 8f). In conclusion, the use of MOFs and COFs as solid electrolytes and separators is a promising strategy for future electrolyte development; however, it is necessary to design rational ion transport channels and new transport mechanisms to ensure the practical ionic conductivity.
## 3 Summary and outlook
In this review, we focused on the current advances in the development of MOF/COF-based materials for ARZIBs, including the material design of $\mathrm{MOFs/COFs}$ , the storage mechanism of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in MOFs/COFs, the use of MOFs/COFs to control the deposition behavior of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , and the use of MOFs/COFs as separators and solid electrolytes.
Although excellent progress has been made, a few challenges remain in the application of MOF/COF-based materials in ARZIBs. First, straightforward methods for the preparation of large-scale MOF-/COF-based materials must be developed to offer more choices. For instance, one-step self-assembly or direct electrodeposition can be used to coat MOFs or COFs on zinc foil [71,82]. Second, investigating the ion storage mechanisms of MOFs and COFs, which is difficult at the atomic level, requires advanced characterization tools and theoretical calculations. Furthermore, in order to deeply explore the stability and phase transition process of MOFs/COFs during battery operation, in-situ techniques for battery studies such as scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), transmission X-ray microscopy (TXM), Raman spectroscopy, and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, are indispensable. Third, the mechanical and chemical stability of MOFs and COFs must be further improved to realize highly durable devices. During the cycling process, the volume distortion resulting from the dissolution of intermediates and ion intercalation during the charge– discharge process negatively affects the stability of MOFs and COFs. Reasonable pore and structure design is necessary to ensure efficient ion transport and accommodate ion intercalation. Fourth, in contrast to the traditional crystalline state, the glass state of MOFs has received considerable attention because of its improved mass transport, mechanical properties, and ability to form grain-boundary-free monoliths [83,84]. However, applications of glass-state MOFs in ARZIBs are still lacking. Fifth, additional effort must be devoted to device-level optimization in more practical scenarios, including those involving large cells, high loading masses, and lean electrolytes.
In conclusion, MOFs and COFs are a promising class of advanced materials for the development of high-performance ARZIBs. Our review systematically describes the most recent developments and applications of MOF-/COF-based materials in ARZIBs. We hope that this review can provide guidance and inspiration for the design and development of MOFs and COFs with novel compositions, structures, and functionalities for high-performance ARZIBs.
